\textbf{1. What Competitive Equilibrium Requires}

For $\bar{x}$ to be a competitive equilibrium (CE) allocation, we require:

\begin{itemize}
    \item There exists a price vector $p$,
    \item Such that, given $p$,
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Each agent maximizes utility subject to their budget constraint,
        \item Markets clear.
    \end{itemize}
\end{itemize}

Formally, for each agent $i$:

\[
\bar{x}^i \in 
\arg\max \left\{ U^i(x^i) \;:\; p \cdot x^i \le p \cdot \omega^i \right\}.
\]

Thus, the relevant question is \emph{not}:

\begin{quote}
Will the other agent agree to trade?
\end{quote}

The correct question is:

\begin{quote}
Given prices $p$, is $\bar{x}^i$ the best bundle that agent $i$ can afford?
\end{quote}

\bigskip

\textbf{2. What Happens in the Picture}

At $\bar{x}$:

\begin{itemize}
    \item The price line passes through $\bar{x}$,
    \item Agent 1 is optimizing (the tangency condition holds),
    \item Agent 2 is \emph{not} optimizing.
\end{itemize}

This means that, although $\bar{x}$ lies on Agent 2's budget line, there exists another affordable bundle that yields strictly higher utility for Agent 2. Therefore, $\bar{x}^2$ is not a solution to Agent 2's maximization problem.

\bigskip

\textbf{3. Clarifying the Intuition}

One might argue:

\begin{quote}
"But agent 1 would refuse to trade, so they remain at $\bar{x}$."
\end{quote}

This reasoning corresponds to the idea of \emph{bilateral voluntary deviation}. However, competitive equilibrium is \emph{not} defined as:

\begin{quote}
No mutually beneficial deviation exists.
\end{quote}

That condition characterizes \textbf{Pareto optimality}.

Competitive equilibrium instead requires:

\begin{quote}
At given prices, each agent independently chooses the best bundle in their budget set.
\end{quote}

Thus, even if no mutually beneficial trade exists, $\bar{x}$ fails to be a competitive equilibrium if some agent can afford and strictly prefers another bundle at the prevailing prices.

The failure is:
At any candidate supporting price, at least one agent has a strictly better affordable bundle.

\begin{center}
    {\LARGE \textcolor{blue}{Introduction}}
\end{center}

\begin{itemize}
    \item Commodities in previous chapter were "private goods": (i) they were consumed by only one person, and (ii) that person’s consumption of the good had no effect on other people’s utility.
\end{itemize}

\noindent
{\large \textcolor{blue}{1.}} Failure of first welfare theorem occurs with \textcolor{red}{externalities}: costs/benefits to third-parties who did not choose to incur such costs/benefits.

\begin{itemize}
    \item Example: smoke in a pub will pollute the environment and lower the utility of every other consumer in the pub.
    \item \textcolor{blue}{The first fundamental theorem assumes that a consumer’s utility is solely a function of that consumer’s consumption bundle.}
\end{itemize}

\noindent
{\large \textcolor{blue}{2.}} CE might deliver Pareto dominated allocations also when there are \textcolor{red}{public goods} (both "non-rivalrous" and "non-exclusive"): the use by one person neither prevents access of other people nor does it fully reduce availability to others. Therefore, the good can be used simultaneously by more than one person.

\begin{itemize}
    \item Examples: television broadcasts (not cable TV); street lighting.
\end{itemize}

\bigskip
\noindent
\textcolor{blue}{Def:} \textit{We are in presence of an externality whenever the well-being of a consumer or the production possibilities of a firm are directly affected by the actions of another agent in the economy.}

